= Cognitive bias — Simple =

A '''cognitive bias''' is a pattern in how people think or judge that can push answers in a predictable direction.

It is not just any mistake. Random mistakes are not necessarily biases.

To call something a bias, you need a standard to compare it with, such as probability, logic, measured facts, or consistent decision rules.

A shortcut in thinking can sometimes cause bias, but shortcuts can also be useful. A fast rule that works well in one situation may fail in another.

Examples include [[Confirmation bias/Simple|confirmation bias]], [[Anchoring effect/Simple|anchoring]], the [[Availability heuristic/Simple|availability heuristic]], and [[Framing effect/Simple|framing effects]].

